    "result_text": "Green \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**Finding 1 (agreed) \u2014 consolidated the crowding threshold.** I reverted the `SOFT_CAPACITY` constant I'd added to `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` and moved the threshold local to `DeriveOwnerRiskFlag`, matching the existing `DeriveOwnerCapacityWarning` idiom (threshold local to the derive class, count/compare inline). Crucially, there is now only **one** literal `40` in the code: `DeriveOwnerRiskFlag.SOFT_CAPACITY = DeriveOwnerCapacityWarning.WARN_THRESHOLD`, so the two signals provably share the same knob and move together. The Javadoc now spells out why the comparisons differ \u2014 the warning marks a city *approaching* the hard cap (band `[40, 50)`, `>=`), while the risk flag fires once a city is *over* the threshold (`> 40`) and stays set up to and at the hard cap. Updated the `Owner.getRiskFlag()` doc cross-reference to the new home.\n\n**Finding 2 (agreed) \u2014 fixed the import idiom.** Added `import java.util.stream.Collectors;` to `ValidateOwnerEmailDomain` and changed the field to `Collectors.toUnmodifiableSet()`, matching how the file imports its other types.\n\nAcceptance behaviour is unchanged (threshold value and `>` comparison are identical to before; only where the constant lives changed), and the suite is green.",
