        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new `DeriveOwnerRiskFlag` class is well-scoped (one clear responsibility, mirrors `DeriveOwnerCapacityWarning`), and reusing `countInCity` / `isDisposableAdjacent` rather than re-deriving those signals is the right call. Two structural issues, though:\n\n**1. City-crowding threshold placement is inconsistent with its sibling, and duplicates the magic number 40 with subtly different semantics.**\n\nThe established pattern for a city-capacity-derived signal is set by `DeriveOwnerCapacityWarning`: it keeps its threshold constant (`WARN_THRESHOLD = 40`) *local to the derive class* and does the count/comparison inline. The new code breaks that pattern by putting `SOFT_CAPACITY = 40` in `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` instead of in `DeriveOwnerRiskFlag`.\n\nThe result is two crowding constants both equal to `40`, living in different classes, with slightly different comparisons:\n- `DeriveOwnerCapacityWarning`: `inCity >= WARN_THRESHOLD (40) && inCity < 50` \u2192 band [40, 50)\n- `DeriveOwnerRiskFlag`: `inCity > CheckOwnerCityCapacity.SOFT_CAPACITY (40)` \u2192 [41, \u221e)\n\nA maintainer reading these will reasonably wonder whether `WARN_THRESHOLD` and `SOFT_CAPACITY` are meant to be the same knob (they're both 40) and whether changing one should change the other \u2014 but they're independently declared in two classes. Pick one home for both crowding thresholds and make the relationship explicit (e.g. both local to their derive classes to match the existing pattern, or both grouped with `CITY_CAPACITY`), and clarify why the two comparisons differ (`>=` vs `>`) rather than leaving two near-identical 40s to reconcile by eye.\n\n**2. Fully-qualified `Collectors` inline breaks the file's import idiom.**\n\nIn `ValidateOwnerEmailDomain`, `DISPOSABLE_BASE_LABELS` uses `java.util.stream.Collectors.toUnmodifiableSet()` fully qualified inline, while the file otherwise imports its types (`java.util.Locale`, `java.util.Set`). Add a normal `import java.util.stream.Collectors;` (and `import java.util.stream.Collectors.*`-style consistency) so the field reads `Collectors.toUnmodifiableSet()` like the rest of the codebase.\n\nNeither is a correctness issue \u2014 the logic sits in sensible units and nothing is a catch-all \u2014 but finding #1 in particular will cost the next maintainer time."
